# Primary Postgres connection string for the API server (production and local dev). ON A DEVELOPER MACHINE THIS IS NOW A READ-ONLY ROLE. Until 2026-09-06 the local value was the `postgres` superuser on the primary — rolsuper, with INSERT and UPDATE on every table — so the "no prod write grant for autonomous sessions" control was procedural only: `operator-db.ts` refused to hand out a write handle, and any script opening this URL with the driver directly bypassed that entirely. The local value is now the `strale_ro` role: SELECT on 41 tables across `public` and `drizzle`, NOSUPERUSER/NOCREATEDB/NOCREATEROLE/ NOBYPASSRLS, plus a role-level `default_transaction_read_only=on`. That last one is defence in depth, not the control — a client can `SET` it off, after which a write still fails with `permission denied for table ...`, which is the actual guarantee. Railway's own variable is UNCHANGED and still writes; the API could not serve traffic otherwise. A non-loopback host MUST carry an explicit `?sslmode=` — `disable` for the Railway public proxy (metro.proxy.rlwy.net terminates no TLS), `require` for a host that does. Operator scripts refuse to open a handle without it, so a value copied verbatim out of Railway (whose internal host needs no suffix) silently disables every local operator script until the suffix is added.
# Railway Postgres usage-based pricing; not a separate line item from compute.
DATABASE_URL=...

# Carries the same explicit `?sslmode=` requirement as DATABASE_URL. Direct (session-mode, unpooled) Postgres connection string for scripts needing session-level features (e.g. advisory locks, LISTEN/NOTIFY) that a pooled DATABASE_URL doesn't support. Read only by an archived diagnostic script.
# Same underlying Railway Postgres instance as DATABASE_URL.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=...

